# A step that writes a table and reads it back only to check it: the count decides whether `keep` fails and goes no
# further, so the table is where the route leaves its notes, in the codomain (mathematics.md, "A route's domain and
# codomain"; rule 4 of "The closed picture is the pushforward of the fine one").

route ledger.run
step run/Ledger()
step run/Ledger.keep
step run/Ledger.keep/connect                                # `con = duckdb.connect(..)` names what it gives

line entry > run call
line run/Ledger() > Ledger@run/Ledger() value
line Ledger@run/Ledger() > run/Ledger.keep value   # its own body changes it (`self.kept +=`) besides handing it to connect
line run/Ledger.keep > Ledger@run/Ledger.keep value         # keep changes the ledger it runs on (`self.kept +=`)
line run/Ledger.keep/connect > DuckDBPyConnection·con@run/Ledger.keep/connect value
line Ledger@run/Ledger() > run/Ledger.keep/connect value   # `duckdb.connect(self.path)` hands the ledger
line DuckDBPyConnection·con@run/Ledger.keep/connect > run/Ledger.keep value   # keep's own calls use con
line run/Ledger.keep > DuckDB(notes) own                    # it inserts the notes; reading them back is no read

home Ledger@run/Ledger() run
home Ledger@run/Ledger.keep run
home DuckDBPyConnection·con@run/Ledger.keep/connect run/Ledger.keep

# An error is a value (#121, decision 4): `RuntimeError(..)` makes it in the arm that raises it, and each border it crosses
# uncaught hands it on, up to the route.
choice run/Ledger.keep/?1
step run/Ledger.keep/?1/RuntimeError()
line run/Ledger.keep/?1/RuntimeError() > RuntimeError@run/Ledger.keep/?1 value
line RuntimeError@run/Ledger.keep/?1 > run/Ledger.keep value
line run/Ledger.keep > RuntimeError@run/Ledger.keep value
line RuntimeError@run/Ledger.keep > run value
home RuntimeError@run/Ledger.keep/?1 run/Ledger.keep
home RuntimeError@run/Ledger.keep run
line run > RuntimeError@run value                           # and the route's border hands it on, as `main`'s does in Rust
home RuntimeError@run run

side entry left
side DuckDB(notes) right

# K: a value handed through a field reaches no step inside: `duckdb.connect(self.path)` hands `self` (#121, decision 1,
#    cause 3: the reader's `held_in` skips `self` even inside an argument)
# building the ledger runs its public methods, which touch the table
